  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let ${S}_{i}=[0+\\frac{1}{i},1],$ then ${\u22c3}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${\u22c3}_{i=3}^{\\infty }$ means that our set of indexes is \n${3,4,5\u2026}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${S}_{3}=[\\frac{1}{3},1]$\n\n- if $i=4,$ we have ${S}_{4}=[\\frac{1}{4},1]$\n\n- if $i=5,$ we have ${S}_{5}=[\\frac{1}{5},1]$\n\n- $\u22ef$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals approaches $0$ but never reaches it, and\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals equals $1$ for all the sets.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c3}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=(0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
